✅ What is a Marker Interface in Java?
A Marker Interface in Java is an empty interface (no methods) that is used to mark a class.
The JVM or framework checks for this “marker” and changes its behavior based on whether the class implements that interface.
In simple terms:
	Marker Interface = a tag that gives special meaning to a class

🔹 Key Characteristics
	• Has no methods
	• Used to provide metadata to JVM or APIs
	• Checked using instanceof or reflection
	• Influences runtime behavior

🔹 Classic Examples in Java
1️⃣ Serializable

public class Employee implements Serializable {
    int id;
    String name;
}
📌 Meaning:
The JVM is allowed to serialize (convert to byte stream) this object.
If a class does not implement Serializable → NotSerializableException is thrown.

2️⃣ Cloneable

public class Student implements Cloneable {
    protected Object clone() throws CloneNotSupportedException {
        return super.clone();
    }
}
📌 Meaning:
Allows the object to be cloned using Object.clone().
Without Cloneable → CloneNotSupportedException.

3️⃣ RandomAccess

List list = new ArrayList<>();
📌 Meaning:
Indicates that the list supports fast (O(1)) random access.
Used internally by algorithms like:

if (list instanceof RandomAccess) {
    // use fast access logic
}

🔹 How Marker Interfaces Work Internally
They are checked at runtime using:
✔ instanceof

if (obj instanceof Serializable) {
    // perform serialization
}
✔ Reflection

obj.getClass().isAssignableFrom(Serializable.class);

🔹 Custom Marker Interface (Example)
Step 1: Create marker interface

public interface Auditable {
}
Step 2: Use it in a class

public class Order implements Auditable {
}
Step 3: Check marker

if (order instanceof Auditable) {
    System.out.println("Enable audit logging");
}

🔹 Marker Interface vs Annotation ⭐ (Interview Favorite)
Marker Interface	Annotation
Empty interface	Uses @ syntax
Checked using instanceof	Checked via reflection
Older Java approach	Modern & flexible
Limited metadata	Can hold values
📌 Modern Java prefers annotations, but marker interfaces are still used in core Java APIs.

🔹 Why Marker Interfaces Exist (Why Not Just Annotations?)
	• Introduced before annotations (Java 5)
	• Allows type-based checking
	• Enables compile-time safety in some cases

🔹 Interview One-Liner
	A marker interface is an empty interface used to mark a class so that JVM or frameworks can apply special behavior to it at runtime.

